              Steinberg ASIO SDK may not be redistributed, and neither
              libjack nor the KS driver is a NimRTC dependency.

============================================================
Sample-rate conversion
============================================================

This product includes software developed by the libsamplerate
contributors (Secret Rabbit Code).
  - Source:   https://github.com/libsndfile/libsamplerate
  - Version:  master @ 0844c208f683527c08ea8a80acc13b398aa9c8bf
              (0.2.2-20-g0844c208; vendored at
               src/third_party/libsamplerate/; pinned SHA in
               src/third_party/vendor.json)
  - License:  BSD-2-Clause  (the full text is in
                   src/third_party/libsamplerate/src/COPYING; the
                   copyright notice is embedded verbatim at the top of
                   every upstream source file)
  - Scope:    runtime, statically linked
              (NIMRTC_VENDORED_LIBSAMPLERATE=ON, the default).  Built
              from source.  Serves as the sample-rate converter behind
              `nimrtc::audio_device::Resampler` (the remote-desktop
              plan's Track F "Resampler" work item, F5).
  - Platforms (cross-platform vendoring policy K8b):
              Windows (MSVC)  — x86-64
              macOS           — arm64 / x86-64
              Linux x86-64    — links libm
              Linux aarch64   — links libm
              libsamplerate is pure C99 over <math.h> with NO
              platform-specific code, so the SAME four upstream
              translation units build on every platform and the
              resampler's output is bit-comparable across them.  The
              x86-64 builds select upstream's SSE2 lrint fast path
              automatically.
  - Note:     The "best" sinc converter is deliberately NOT built —
              its coefficient table is a ~9 MB array of doubles that
              costs a great deal of compile time for a converter a
              remote desktop never selects.  The "fast" and "medium"
              converters are built.  No system samplerate library is
              ever linked (L5): with the vendored switch off the
              module uses its pure-C++ linear-interpolation fallback.

